To create an interactive website with a colorful interface, you can use HTML for structure and CSS for styling, and JavaScript for interactivity. Below is a simple example of how you might do this.

HTML (`index.html`):
```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="UTF-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
<title>My Joke Website</title>
<link rel="stylesheet" href="styles.css">
</head>
<body>
<div class="container">
    <h1>My Joke Website</h1>
    <div class="joke-container">
        <p id="joke">[Funny Joke]</p>
        <button onclick="revealPunchline()">Push to reveal punchline</button>
    </div>
</div>

<script src="script.js"></script>
</body>
</html>
```

CSS (`styles.css`):
```css
body {
    font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
    background-color: #f0f0f0;
    padding-top: 50px;
}

.container {
    width: 300px;
    margin: auto;
    border: 2px solid #333;
    padding: 20px;
    box-shadow: 0 0 10px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1);
    background-color: #fff;
}

h1 {
    color: #4CAF50; /* Green color */
}

.joke-container {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
}

#joke {
    color: #333;
    font-style: italic;
    transition: all 0.5s ease;
}

button {
    border: none;
    padding: 10px 20px;
    background-color: #4CAF50; /* Green color */
    color: white;
    font-size: 16px;
    cursor: pointer;
}
```

JavaScript (`script.js`):
```javascript
function revealPunchline() {
    const jokeElement = document.getElementById('joke');
    const jokeText = "Why don't scientists trust atoms? Because they make up everything.";
    jokeElement.innerHTML = jokeText;
    document.getElementById('joke').style.color = "#FF5733"; /* Orange color for the punchline */
}
```

This example creates a simple but interactive joke
